In Nairobi County today, clean water is a privilege, not a right. Thousands of families in peri-urban and informal settlements buy water from private vendors at five times the municipal rate — or walk kilometers to the nearest public tap. Bill Llia's **Water for All** plan aims to end that within three years.

## The plan — four fronts, one goal

1. **Borehole rehabilitation** — Over 40% of county-owned boreholes sit broken and abandoned. The plan repairs every single one within the first year and establishes a dedicated maintenance unit to prevent future neglect.

2. **Pipeline expansion** — 200 kilometers of new pipeline in two years, targeting underserved wards that currently depend on expensive water trucking.

3. **Community water kiosks** — 500 new kiosks installed in partnership with community organizations, delivering clean water at or below KES 5 per 20-liter jerrycan, within 500 meters of every household.

4. **Rainwater harvesting** — Subsidies and technical assistance for households and schools to install catchment systems, reducing grid dependence and building climate resilience across the county.

## The cost — and the return

Estimated investment: KES 4.5 billion over three years, funded through county budget allocation, national government grants, and development partner financing. The return? Healthier families, more productive hours, and savings that stay in household budgets instead of going to water vendors.

"Every shilling spent on water infrastructure is an investment in health, dignity, and economic productivity," Bill said. "When a mother no longer spends two hours fetching water, she spends that time working, learning, or caring for her children. That is the return we are after."

## Grace's story — the reason this matters

At the Mathare announcement, resident Grace Wanjiku spoke plainly: "I wake up at 4 AM every day to queue for water. Some days the vendor does not come, and we pay five times the price from private sellers. I want my children to turn on a tap and water comes out."

"Grace's story is the story of millions of Nairobi residents," Bill responded. "And it is a story we will change — together."

## What this means for you

If you live in an underserved ward, this plan determines whether 2027 is another year of dawn queues and expensive jerrycans — or the year a tap finally works in your neighborhood. The question is not whether the resources exist. The question is whether the next Governor has the will to deploy them.
